BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Archive-Frictions-Banner-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Symposium,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T094017Z
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the opening of Archive Frictions\, a Gallery-on-Demand exhibition at the Sasken Multimedia Gallery. The exhibition will be inaugurated with a public symposium exploring women’s work and presence through photographic archives.\n\nAt the heart of Archive Frictions is a remarkable archive of photographs discovered by PhD researcher Alexandra Gow at the National Galleries Scotland. Believed to have been taken in the Indian subcontinent in the early to mid-1900s\, the photographs offer a unique glimpse of women in university settings. In contrast to many stereotypical representations of India from the period\, these images focus on women in educational spaces and are marked by an unusually intimate photographic style.\n\nTwo contemporary artists — Priyadarshini Ravichandran\, based in India\, and Steffi Klenz\, based in the UK\, have responded to the archive through the creation of new visual works. In doing so\, Archive Frictions fosters a dynamic dialogue between historical and contemporary perspectives\, illuminating the evolving representation of women in photography across time and cultural contexts.\n\n&nbsp\;\n\nPlease find more details about the symposium and launch below:\n\nDate: September 13\, 2026\n\nTime: 03:00 - 06:30 PM\n\nSchedule:\n\n03:00 - 03:30 PM: An Introduction to Fast Forward &amp\; Archive Frictions by Anna Fox\n\n03:30 - 04:00 PM: Launch and walkthrough of Archive Frictions \n\n04:30 - 05:15 PM: Presentation by Suryanandini Narain\n\nThis talk will discuss two photographic albums by Mary Louisa Booth (1896-1939)\, titled the Antiquities of Kathiawad (c. 1910) and The Watson Memorial Institute\, Rajkot (1893). Together\, the albums are testament to the Raj’s interest in India’s pre-colonial archaeology\, as well as its civic modernisation under the guidance of the British government. Mary Louisa Booth’s albums possibly make her the first woman photographer in the field of Indian archaeology.\n\n05:30 - 06:30 PM: Archive Frictions: Contemporary Interpretations\n\nThis discussion will feature artists Priyadarshini Ravichandran and Steffi Klenz in conversation with Mira Brunner regarding their creative responses to the archive and their practices at large.\n\n&nbsp\;\n\nAbout the speakers:\n\nAnna Fox is an acclaimed British photographer and Professor of Photography at the University for the Creative Arts. Working in colour\, Fox first gained attention for Work Stations: Office Life in London (1988)\, a study of office culture in Thatcher’s Britain. She is best known for Zwarte Piet (1993-8)\, a series of portraits taken over five years that explore Dutch black-face folk traditions associated with Christmas. Fox is currently working on the research project Fast Forward: Women in Photography\, for which she has been awarded a Leverhulme International Networks Grant\, an AHRC Engagement Fellowship and an AHRC Follow on Award\, as well as being supported in China\, the UK\, Nigeria and India by the British Council. \n\nSuryanandini Narain is Assistant Professor in Visual Studies at the School of Arts and Aesthetics\, Jawaharlal Nehru University. Her research and critical writing focus on photography\, visual culture and aesthetics. Her recent scholarly work includes co-editing Framing Portraits\, Binding Albums: Family Photographs in India (Zubaan\, 2024). \n\nSteffi Klenz is a London-based artist who approaches photography as an expanded visual discipline. Her work has been exhibited widely across the UK\, including at the Royal Academy\, the British Museum\, the Wellcome Collection and Camden Art Centre in London\, and the Royal Scottish Academy in Edinburgh. Internationally\, her work has been shown at the Museum of Contemporary Art Taipei\, the Finnish Museum of Photography in Helsinki\, Künstlerhaus Bethanien in Berlin\, CICA Museum in South Korea and FotoMuseum Antwerp. She has participated in major international exhibitions and festivals\, including the British Council exhibition presented alongside Documenta in Athens (2017)\, the Biennale for Contemporary Photography in Germany (2020)\, the International Biennale for Photography in Belo Horizonte (2021)\, the Tokyo Biennale (2023)\, Bodø European Capital of Culture (2024) and the International Biennale of Contemporary Art in Uzbekistan (2024).\n\nPriyadarshini Ravichandran is a photographer from Tamil Nadu working closely with recurrent themes emerging in relationships that root\, reveal or unsettle her. She is compelled to tell stories of women\, our lives and the land. She seeks to collaborate with researchers\, friends\, institutions\, trees and animals- placing her in unexpected intersections. She received the Parasol Prize from the V&amp\;A Museum (2023) and the Quai Branly Museum award(2024).\n\nMira Brunner is a Bengaluru-based artist\, archivist\, and writer. She works across media\, is interested in deep time and epistemology\, and is informed by queer &amp\; disability studies. She has exhibited her work at the Museum of Art and Photography\, Bengaluru (2026)\, 24 Hours Neukölln\, Berlin (2026)\, the Chennai Photo Biennale\, Chennai\, (2025)\, neunKelche e.V.\, Berlin (2024)\, Dekalb Gallery\, Brooklyn (2024)\, and University of the Arts\, London (2023). Her writing has been published in Lassitude Zine\, Sick Magazine\, The Asian Journal of Women’s Studies\, and Out of Print Magazine. On weekdays\, she’s the chief archivist at QAMRA – the Queer Archive for Memory\, Reflection\, and Activism.\n\n&nbsp\;\n\nArchive Frictions is delivered in collaboration between Museum of Art and Photography (MAP)\, in Bengaluru\, the National Galleries Scotland (NGS)\, in Edinburgh\, and Fast Forward: Women in Photography. It is supported by the University of Creative Arts and British Council.\n\nSupported By\n\n
DTEND:20260913T130000Z
DTSTART:20260913T093000Z
SUMMARY:Archive Frictions
UID:1389@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/archive-frictions/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Death_Rituals_Lagori_Collective.png
CATEGORIES:Workshops,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T124316Z
DESCRIPTION:Image Credits: Lagori Collective\nDeath is the moment where the question of plural worlds becomes most personal. Every culture has answered it differently. Some say you move to another world\, some say you dissolve back into this one and some say you join a world of ancestors running alongside the living. There are lots of different understandings of what the world is\, who inhabits it\, and what it means to leave it.\n\nMost of us have inherited rituals for death without choosing them. And most of those rituals were designed only for humans. This workshop asks: what if we designed them ourselves for every world\, every species\, every relationship that ends? \n\nUsing a ritual design framework created by Lagori Collective\, participants will be invited to sit with death as a transition between worlds and to begin designing the ritual that would mark that crossing on their own terms.\n\nThis event is part of Plural Worlds\, developed in collaboration with Lagori Collective and presented in conjunction with Beneath the Turning Sky.\n\n&nbsp\;
DTEND:20260905T103000Z
DTSTART:20260905T083000Z
SUMMARY:Rituals for Dying Into Other Worlds
UID:1390@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/rituals-for-dying-into-other-worlds/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/D70-sofa-by-Tecno-design-Osvaldo-Borsani.jpg
CATEGORIES:Talks,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T093913Z
DESCRIPTION:Studio Ballo+Ballo\, 1954\, Divano D70\, Osvaldo Borsani for Tecno\nJoin us for a talk with historian and curator Libby Sellers exploring how twentieth-century design became iconic not simply through the objects themselves\, but through the visual ecosystems that surrounded them: photography\, magazines\, catalogues\, advertising\, exhibitions\, museums and collecting. \n\nBeginning with John Berger’s Ways of Seeing and the idea that every image embodies a particular way of seeing\, the talk considers how images have shaped our understanding of what design looks like\, what it means\, and which objects and designers become part of the cultural canon.\n\nFrom post-war Italian design through Californian architecture\, it asks how photography transforms both the built environment and our understanding of it. The talk then brings this framework into a South Asian context through the architect Minnette de Silva and the journal MARG\, exploring the role of publishing in making a regional modernism visible and intelligible\, while opening questions around gender\, authorship and the construction of the design canon. Drawing on research into twentieth-century furniture\, women designers and collectible design\, the talk asks who gets photographed\, published\, collected and remembered\, and who remains outside the frame. \n\nUltimately\, the talk asks: when we think we recognise an iconic object\, are we recognising the object itself\, or the history of images that has taught us how to see it?
DTEND:20260925T123000Z
DTSTART:20260925T113000Z
SUMMARY:How Objects Become Icons: Photography\, Publishing and the Making of Design History
UID:1395@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/how-objects-become-icons-photography-publishing-and-the-making-of-design-history/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/875081a2-2dc2-4d8d-b8d8-71969bb71471.webp
CATEGORIES:Game,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260831T120416Z
DESCRIPTION:What if the artworks in a museum's collection became the building blocks of your own universe? In this session designed by Abhishek Lamba\, a single deck of cards unlocks three different games — each one inviting you to draw on works from the collection\, and use them as material for chance\, strategy\, and imagination. Come play\, build\, and see the collection in a way you never have before.\n\nThis event is part of Plural Worlds\, developed in collaboration with Lagori Collective and presented in conjunction with Beneath the Turning Sky.
DTEND:20260906T123000Z
DTSTART:20260906T103000Z
SUMMARY:The Universe
UID:1392@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/the-universe/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/CY102132-1-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Panel Discussion,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T123340Z
DESCRIPTION:A moderated conversation exploring knowledge systems that often remain outside textbooks\, including community ecologies\, urban land reading\, and cultural practice. Moving through three questions—what it means to know the world from where one stands and whose standing has historically counted\; what knowledge about the natural world exists in the city that dominant frameworks cannot see\; and what might change in how the future is imagined if these other ways of knowing were taken seriously—the session invites audiences to briefly inhabit a world other than the one they walked in with.\n\nThis event is part of Plural Worlds\, developed in collaboration with Lagori Collective and presented in conjunction with Beneath the Turning Sky.
DTEND:20260905T130000Z
DTSTART:20260905T110000Z
SUMMARY:How Do We Know The Universe?
UID:1388@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/how-do-we-know-the-universe/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Assitive-Tech-2.jpg
CATEGORIES:Mixer,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T090226Z
DESCRIPTION:Art\, Technology &amp\; Access: Rethinking the Museum Experience\n\nHow can technology create new ways of experiencing\, participating in and accessing art?\n\nMaking Art\, Making Access brings together artists\, accessibility practitioners\, technology professionals and museums to explore how technology is changing the ways we create and experience art—and what this could mean for accessibility in cultural spaces.\n\nArtists are increasingly using digital tools and technology to challenge conventional ideas of participation. For museums\, these practices open up new possibilities: Can technology offer audiences more choice in how they encounter art? How can museums adopt and adapt these possibilities to create more inclusive experiences?\n\nThe afternoon moves from artistic practice to technology\, universal design principle\, possibility and action. Hear from an artist on how technology and adaptation can transform artistic practice. Explore Bloomberg Connects and how shared digital infrastructure can help museums create more accessible pathways to collections and interpretation. \n\nThe schedule will be released soon.
DTEND:20260912T130000Z
DTSTART:20260912T083000Z
SUMMARY:Making Art\, Making Access
UID:1394@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/making-art-making-access/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-28-165612.png
CATEGORIES:Workshops,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260828T112650Z
DESCRIPTION:We all carry maps of the places we know: the street we take home\, the room we grew up in\, the neighbourhoods we navigate every day. But what do these maps leave out? What might happen if we mapped not only buildings and roads\, but also water\, heat\, birds\, memories\, labour\, decay\, waiting\, migration\, infrastructure\, and imagined futures? What if a familiar place could be encountered as one that holds several worlds at once?\nFacilitated by Ishita Jain from Living Midnight Narrative Outfit\, participants will choose a familiar and intimate place and begin to map its invisible territories. Through drawing\, tracing\, layering\, and juxtaposition\, they will explore its environmental\, biological\, social\, infrastructural\, and temporal layers\, while considering the futures that might already be latent within these relationships.\nAs the maps take shape\, they may begin to move beyond conventional representations of place\, becoming fields of movement\, encounters\, and possibilities. The aim is not to create a more accurate map of a familiar world\, but to explore what becomes visible when the way of seeing it changes.\n\nThis event is part of Plural Worlds\, developed in collaboration with Lagori Collective and presented in conjunction with Beneath the Turning Sky.\n\n&nbsp\;
DTEND:20260906T110000Z
DTSTART:20260906T083000Z
SUMMARY:Counter Mapping: Local Realities
UID:1391@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/counter-mapping-local-realities/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/IMG-20260510-WA0158-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Walkthroughs,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T124230Z
DESCRIPTION:Image Credits: Vidit Jain\nThis walk invites participants to move slowly\, attending closely to the many worlds that coexist within the same stretch of the city: ecological\, historical\, multispecies\, and everyday. Walking then becomes a way of sensing these plural worlds and the futures already being rehearsed within them.\n\nSimple prompts will guide attention along the way: What exists here that no map would show? Whose world is this\, and whose has it been? What futures are already present in this neighbourhood\, waiting to be noticed?\n\nThe walk begins at MAP and moves through Cubbon Park before concluding at the Central State Library. Along the route\, participants will sense and gather local\, place-based imaginations of the future\, considering urban ecologies\, recreation\, local economies\, and community.\n\nThe session will close with a collective reflection\, inviting participants to ask: What worlds did we walk through\, and which ones were invisible to us before today?\n\nThis event is part of Plural Worlds\, developed in collaboration with Lagori Collective and presented in conjunction with Beneath the Turning Sky.
DTEND:20260905T063000Z
DTSTART:20260905T043000Z
SUMMARY:Futures Walk
UID:1387@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/futures-walk/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/POP.23535.3.jpg
CATEGORIES:Book Launch,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T111419Z
DESCRIPTION:What can a lobby card tell us about the ways we have imagined\, remembered and encountered Indian cinema?\n\nMarking the launch of the publication ‘The Momentary and the Monumental’\, this panel brings together contributors Ashish Rajadhyaksha\, Debashree Mukherjee and Iyesha Geeth Abbas to reflect on the histories\, aesthetics and material lives of popular cinema publicity. Moving beyond the films themselves\, the conversation will consider lobby cards and related ephemera as carefully constructed visual objects—shaped by photographers\, artists\, designers\, printers and the conditions of their circulation.\n\nDrawing on their contributions to the publication\, the speakers will explore what these often-overlooked images reveal about the visual language of Indian cinema\, the cultures of print and publicity that surrounded it\, and the ways in which cinematic memory is produced beyond the screen. The discussion will also open up questions around the archives and histories that remain to be uncovered\, and what it means to look closely at the material traces of popular cinema.\n\n&nbsp\;
DTEND:20260911T140000Z
DTSTART:20260911T130000Z
SUMMARY:The Momentary and the Monumental - Publication Launch
UID:1396@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/the-momentary-and-the-monumental-publication-launch/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/04-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Youth Engagement,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260901T090202Z
DESCRIPTION:Drawing from MAP’s textile collection\, this workshop explores how textiles can carry memories through colour\, material and repair. Using a curated selection of objects\, including kantha\, patchwork\, appliqué\, quilting\, embroidered purses\, shawls\, saris and shade cards\, participants will closely observe the traces of making\, mending\, wear and care embedded within each object.\n\nBeginning with observation\, participants will reflect on the personal associations these textiles evoke\, whether it is a grandmother’s quilt\, a mother’s sari\, a well-worn shawl or a favourite piece of clothing. These memories become a starting point for developing a visual vocabulary of colours\, marks and rhythms that responds to the material qualities of the objects rather than reproducing them.\n\nRegistration for the event will open soon!\n\nThis programme is exclusively for 18–25 year olds.\n\nThis session is conducted as part of our engagements for the MAP Youth Collective. The MAP Youth Collective is a community for young people (18-25 year olds) to engage with the arts and culture sector. The collective is for\, of and by young people — to explore our shared identities and interests through art.
DTEND:20260913T073000Z
DTSTART:20260913T053000Z
SUMMARY:Tracing Color
UID:1393@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/tracing-color/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR